#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162857} :activity/id #uuid "5f2d7b62-9c95-5591-9da4-e8e5d9022441" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050726 :topic/id #uuid "61838998-9541-5ba0-829a-1421ece4cbfb" :topic/math-academy-id 3132} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162857 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163232 :tutorial/content "The **singular value decomposition** (or SVD) of an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ is a factorization of the form\n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is an $m \\times n$ matrix with the singular values of $A$ (ordered from largest to smallest) on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m \\times m$ matrix, and\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n \\times n$ matrix.\n\nThe columns of $U$ are called the **left singular vectors** of $A,$ and the columns of $V$ are called the **right singular vectors** of $A.$\n\nFor example, the matrix ![](images/46/46b29ceb84e2ed98e48642e67277c9bf63d0552bcd07f813a9ffcaf665ff91b7.svg) has the following singular value decomposition:\n\n![](images/29/298933eb0a789ebe57a296088172f6927d1573770824c0b0cffb393eb9894c70.svg)\n\nThe singular value decomposition of a matrix $A$ can be thought of as a type of diagonalization that works for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix! It's also one of linear algebra's most useful forms of matrix factorization with applications in statistics, image compression, machine learning, and more." :tutorial/id #uuid "ae8b1a4c-c37e-5527-9f72-9cf20f5af926" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4892 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "db915660-02e7-5288-8506-ad3f2aa2ddf5" :step/math-academy-id 30547 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162908}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161808 :question/id #uuid "232a0173-d743-5696-a6d9-c8279514bcba" :question/math-academy-id "e-8413"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c94ec9c4-650f-5d39-9294-651b03b172bf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050205}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418159}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "486b32a1-b52c-5baa-bdcc-412851ba4a9e" :step/math-academy-id 31341 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162913}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162017 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161809 :question/id #uuid "ad5570e6-4810-5b07-a676-711f0fd069a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-8414"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0d7f5df-a1f9-5242-9386-446313ec0178" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418158}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "3b004c12-845e-56ca-9f93-dabde8cab0c8" :step/math-academy-id 31342 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162912}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162910 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162018 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161810 :question/id #uuid "d861ebc5-8e1e-5cf0-9b82-5a64ff80cf96" :question/math-academy-id "e-8415"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02dfe937-e604-5985-af6f-cdfa1a574ccd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048493}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428015}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Columns of the Third Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "3da79aa6-a10d-52b5-a714-6b64af170db3" :step/math-academy-id 31343 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162914}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162911 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162019 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161811 :question/id #uuid "828e62a9-0076-5ff8-8220-4f779d48eb65" :question/math-academy-id "e-8416"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9466d5b-d361-5504-8e16-99f87210e808" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418154}] :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "5eeddc56-d36b-512a-a9d9-5d2227617238" :step/math-academy-id 31344 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163155}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162912 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163246 :tutorial/content "We can now fully describe the algorithm for computing the singular value decomposition of $A.$\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A.$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values. This allows us to construct the second matrix $\\Sigma.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$). This allows us to construct the third matrix $V.$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ using the singular values, right singular vectors, and the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively. This allows us to construct the matrix $U.$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $\\Sigma$ shown below. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ let's find the matrices $U$ and $V.$\n\n![](images/ab/abe87fb2a304ff102bb3f703d025c7d41f56cb6fab7fc477b3073cbd820cda6e.svg)\n\nWe find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using our algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & =[\\begin{aligned}89 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 113\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\color{blue}\\sigma_1=11$ and $\\color{red}\\sigma_2=9.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T \\! A - 121I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives\n![](images/41/4195807c3f61cd730c57340666c8d651e94d2b277f9623da7cd482c1d7123303.svg)\n\n- Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T \\! A - 81I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives\n![](images/64/64aa31f039ec188355522cffa8a6c62df8c53bb409e55df32ce880d9a9efcd6e.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ab/ab0a5a5a284c962414158e5b7c463264b271bdd57decd8e08305285f7243f7bd.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "4b48ac70-7c4e-59be-bdfb-64125ae50eec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5010 :tutorial/title "An Algorithm for Finding a Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "c0a6dc33-d7cd-5300-a862-451162ab2de3" :step/math-academy-id 31345 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162911}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162913 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163247 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix, and \n\n$$\nA= U\\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A.$ Let's describe how to find the matrices $V$ and $\\Sigma$ of this singular value decomposition. \n\nNotice that if we compute $A^TA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={(UΣ{V}^{T})}^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =(V{Σ}^{T}\\,{U}^{T})(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}\\,U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}({I}_{n})Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V({Σ}^{T}\\,Σ){V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we note that $A^T \\! A$ is always symmetric, and that $\\Sigma^T \\! \\Sigma$ is a diagonal matrix with the squares of the singular values on the main diagonal. As a result,\n\n$$\nA^TA = V\\left( \\Sigma^T \\Sigma\\right) V^T\n$$\n\nis an orthogonal diagonalization of the symmetric matrix $A^T \\! A.$ So, to find the matrix $V$ in our SVD, we need to orthogonally diagonalize $A^T A.$\n\nLet's demonstrate how this works in practice by computing the matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V$ in the SVD of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/b7/b766ec7d8c00dfc121e707566d21db470c087c3e785f4e1b2868e7f932873c67.svg)\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- Computing $A^T \\! A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving the characteristic equation $|A^T \\! A - \\lambda I| = 0,$ we get the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\lambda_1 = 16}}, \\qquad  {\\color{red}{\\lambda_2 = 1}}\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding singular values are \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}  = 4},  \\qquad {\\color{red}\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2} = 1}.\n$$\n\nWe now find a unit eigenvector basis of $A^T \\! A.$ These unit eigenvectors will be the columns of our matrix $V$ in the orthogonal diagonalization of $A^T \\! A$.\n\n- Computing $(A^T \\! A - {\\color{blue}\\lambda_1} I),$ we obtain\n![](images/f0/f074943374fd488663eda967ab64f335047f9b6cc91c98c504dcb0f2ee8282a6.svg)\nSeeking a unit solution of $(A^T\\! A - 16I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},$ we get\n![](images/03/03d6a116d751736f50c366cd4721ee7d1dbf39048092272acc6577934f4600c7.svg)\n\n- Computing $(A^T \\! A - {\\color{red}\\lambda_2} I),$ we obtain\n![](images/16/16813fa19b3417b6de49f9c3c1c27cfedca850b473da01d3b0e0a0b3091b6e28.svg)\nSeeking a unit solution of $(A^T\\! A - I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},$ we get\n![](images/32/32b3c9740740767d4d849ac2d73552dee1e225cc507e95a17c727e8c3fcc6275.svg)\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/85/85c926521f0588117992c019ab0db3723520a14ba4c2879d630ab391462b4af2.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "bbaa68b2-45d9-56a5-99b7-90dce5ec08da" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5011 :tutorial/title "Finding the Second and Third Matrices of a Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "c7ff20bf-405e-5d22-a1a1-24febcd398fc" :step/math-academy-id 31348 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162910}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162914 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163248 :tutorial/content "Suppose that ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is an ![](images/25/2521651122403a440e682fef383af333958f5b0d1334abe8b0771865d4daf100.svg) matrix, and \n\n![](images/ec/ec033e8518a97ff363494df7effa3c6f032b7cfaa028712b87959d659ea12962.svg)\nis a singular value decomposition of ![](images/c8/c8c72090d28b539bed2177052b06540383d63ac720008f1b301dafcac4fa6227.svg) Let's now describe how to compute the matrix ![](images/c3/c3ae10e66e0dee45c359329a1cd8b0246634f95fdce2f5bf206d04dac3594fc5.svg) of the SVD using the matrices ![](images/12/128e4b474ccbb86ef9b1a105a348e493595f4084d05e9cfa5f7889d390d5eac4.svg) and ![](images/4d/4d936a1af541b15fe85bff8b60fbe1d8d1851f9e91616cf4799dd0c5323d038a.svg)\n\nRecall that ![](images/be/bef27a67f1f86637c8224c0d929308a97b23aad62dfbbc5549c68e12bb94f143.svg) is orthogonal, meaning that ![](images/c7/c71eaab408a38ca1a1d0c653fe51ab02287d8ffbe4ef0461c93186579552d456.svg) Consequently, multiplying ![](images/c9/c99b0facd0ff831056deed9aaa5356ca5cbeed72ed9f632604cd5fb9479c3b7a.svg) by ![](images/be/bef27a67f1f86637c8224c0d929308a97b23aad62dfbbc5549c68e12bb94f143.svg) on the right, we obtain\n\n![](images/03/036ae766d4187370271793b6bbacc9841b444173ff031740acbd259531ed5126.svg)\n\nIn expanded form, our final equation looks as follows:\n\n![](images/b9/b924929217b058d3edefff9a6557d3ad8b5d6b3536e7ac0dcaf185e50b60bb5a.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](images/64/64b1897c8532aee8fee0d4f76f632cc91c6a5a4078b11bfaaea07235aeadcc3f.svg)\n\nfor ![](images/fe/fedbe16eb8c09802bf435c256bb225249c1ee578dd558d0f3b6b284a56236517.svg) Finally, provided that ![](images/66/668ce05b1a81d3d120c47a7dadea446891ef986edb5f499c592f310755bb143c.svg) we obtain \n\n![](images/ec/ecd9fe71eaef2172512d70bfb78ffea6e36b973e6f96c09f0480ef2e0e9e55ad.svg) \n\nWe can use this to find the columns of the matrix ![](images/f9/f9d412fae57d02f4fc44b04a277e1a6512e3f560e3152416a69d30d72e38a9ac.svg) Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "b577630f-1060-52c1-aabe-91b641046fa5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5012 :tutorial/title "Finding the First Matrix of a Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "e1d953a4-69ca-563a-afef-090b7f058ef3" :step/math-academy-id 31349 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163053}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163053 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163317 :tutorial/content "Consider the matrices $A,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V$ shown below. \n\n![](images/61/61ff6d3a83c2a660ca8369d96ab7bede86f9a124727db8d5a7e320dcd5aaad74.svg)\n\nSuppose we're given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A.$ Let's use this information to find the matrix $U.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\color{blue}\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\color{red}\\sigma_2=1.$ \n\nEarlier, we saw that the columns of $\\mathbf u$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively. Therefore, we compute the columns of $\\mathbf u$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute the vector $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the vector $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the matrix $U$ in our singular value decomposition of $A$ is given by\n\n![](images/15/159ace613dacf666b32131543c77b3eb602e16226ba2d37e9e8bea4dcff1cb56.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "04644a93-8cce-5c2e-b575-d7f05b4985df" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6135 :tutorial/title "Calculating the First Matrix - A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "9c32bd79-d939-5ba0-afca-75b88e74b8b9" :step/math-academy-id 37257 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162909}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163155 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163364 :tutorial/content "Let $A = U \\Sigma V^T$ be a singular value decomposition of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ with singular values $\\sigma_1$ and $\\sigma_2.$ \n\nDue to the decomposition, the action of $A$ on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is the same as the action of the sequence $V^T \\to \\Sigma \\to U.$\n\nAs shown below, let's consider both equivalent actions on a unit circle (with the two standard basis vectors). \n\n![](images/18/18bf28d59138e6c50e950493570a4e9caa8ec2f86eb81d573272cb4677ea1d69.png)\n\n- Since $V^T$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix, it represents a transformation of the plane that preserves distances between vectors. This could be a rotation, a reflection, or a combination of these two, which is sometimes called a **rotoreflection.**\n\n- Since $\\Sigma$ is a diagonal matrix, it represents a scaling along the $x$- and $y$-axes by $\\sigma_1$ and $\\sigma_2,$ respectively.\n\n- Since $U$ is again an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix, it represents a rotation, a reflection, or a rotoreflection.\n\nTo summarize, we have the following theorem:\n\n*Any linear transformation on the plane can be decomposed into an orthogonal transformation (rotation, reflection, or rotoreflection), a scaling, and another orthogonal transformation (rotation, reflection, or rotoreflection).*" :tutorial/id #uuid "4cadc01a-8396-532a-a438-479affeb9a04" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7033 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "7cc03796-6cdf-582b-9c6a-edc187a827b2" :step/math-academy-id 42397}] :activity/title "Singular Value Decomposition of 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162226}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428020} :activity/id #uuid "be1bd01d-d4e3-5a46-9543-53b198bb6268" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050726 :topic/id #uuid "61838998-9541-5ba0-829a-1421ece4cbfb" :topic/math-academy-id 3132} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428016 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b340a6b9-2150-5d09-b212-4169a2e45650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371362} {:answer/id #uuid "ed71c6ee-fc7e-5366-b928-3a0e8ac31c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371363} {:answer/id #uuid "3decd7ef-d6cb-5d2c-a821-182982c2d6d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371364} {:answer/id #uuid "f1659e51-bced-5253-9862-0e84267f1efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371365} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7eb61e-6225-5628-bd68-d73407f01588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371366}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371365} :answer-field/id #uuid "64e997c7-7524-5cf1-946e-c03af754bbbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9e6bed9f-9e8e-54a8-ba26-f0e8954ce7e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-96303" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0c463439-a38b-593e-b012-0bd4d757c8b7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428019}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428017 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e43e58b-e171-5bde-8e66-fa1047e3a5e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404838} {:answer/id #uuid "ad42d289-8707-56c1-bf83-45db2fe36ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404839} {:answer/id #uuid "999d413b-60b1-5bd7-abe7-54bb8a4e91b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404840} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe630aa-18c2-5c17-a5c7-5d9c6cf67fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404841} {:answer/id #uuid "7758da6c-c911-54cb-96cc-7117ea4fabca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404842}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404839} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca27652a-1905-5111-863a-956b813d6f8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "069d96cb-6a82-58f7-9653-ba67b55165b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-95912" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=4$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-16I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "42130988-8a9c-5c98-b57a-c5c217558433" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428018}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428018 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428015 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "089ec07a-5e86-5e2c-9ee0-7a59f0e9ce6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186428009} {:answer/id #uuid "7011776a-f74f-5038-88b7-bd75d4a9eff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186428010} {:answer/id #uuid "7450dd95-1930-5564-96d4-6c9e274f1579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186428011} {:answer/id #uuid "a9766852-3203-5031-bf4e-845ae1f644cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186428012} {:answer/id #uuid "da310c2d-2eb6-5830-855e-93aa19083cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186428013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428010} :answer-field/id #uuid "01f21526-a5f6-5403-8d94-60d82378f21e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186428014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "39a1a8bd-f69f-5f36-99bc-5bd95f9b782d" :question/math-academy-id "q-96238" :question/problem "$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}73 & 24 \\\\ 24 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=9.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=81.$ Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-81I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-81I=[\\begin{aligned}73 & 24 \\\\ 24 & 9\\end{aligned}]-81[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 24 \\\\ 24 & -72\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-81I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}|=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b772e15d-f5b9-5724-9449-b771545c21bc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428016}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428019 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404891 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4eaa68b-520c-58b4-a17b-57dd48766c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404893} {:answer/id #uuid "dad029c8-f1bd-5774-bd9b-6cc44666c74f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404894} {:answer/id #uuid "b1179464-c161-5f17-9d1b-ec79fe124e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404895} {:answer/id #uuid "13acfcf7-36f6-5edb-88df-5d728cfbb0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404896} {:answer/id #uuid "717ea9f0-7aa4-55d1-823a-4458379130c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404897}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404896} :answer-field/id #uuid "e29ceeea-5603-57fa-99fc-57e0cab72178" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5095a12c-e6f9-56a4-8ce5-1c1e4698b3dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-96360" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}65 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 80\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=8.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=81,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-81I & =[\\begin{aligned}65 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 80\\end{aligned}]-81[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-81I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=64,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-64I & =[\\begin{aligned}65 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 80\\end{aligned}]-64[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-64I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{32}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bc5679a7-ffcd-561e-8c7e-fd7ddec627e4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428020 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e784017-9f26-57fb-8d09-e2c85b00c13e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371285} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fd7571-0b27-57c2-bd8b-9ae270401cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371286} {:answer/id #uuid "053e86da-1624-5e0f-a032-984baf156135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371287} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7c0920-0814-53cc-a6b5-0e0aded26986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371288} {:answer/id #uuid "52d8a537-0c37-5e79-800b-5a598b1c9e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371289}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371287} :answer-field/id #uuid "06d08e03-206a-52a5-b561-b040a6082be8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7f6a8969-0c0e-547e-babb-6ff2c612e774" :question/math-academy-id "q-95903" :question/problem "$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}68 & 26 \\\\ 26 & 29\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ So, the second column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=16.$ Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-16I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-16I=[\\begin{aligned}68 & 26 \\\\ 26 & 29\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}52 & 26 \\\\ 26 & 13\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}|+|\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}|=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eb2350bf-19b6-5869-b8de-89f0f6ea18f9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428017}}] :activity/title "Singular Value Decomposition of 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186428008}]}
